course: dictionary
lesson: 6954
title: One word, two meanings: palaver
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. «What a palaver just to get a passport!» Here the speaker is annoyed by all the bother, so palaver means a fuss about something small.
explain: In old books, a palaver is a long talk or a meeting where people talk things over. Look at the words around it: if people are sitting down to discuss something, the old meaning fits. Always try your meaning in the sentence and check it still makes sense.
explain: The word is informal, so you will hear it in chatty talk more than in school essays. Good readers notice the tone too: a palaver sounds a bit cross, as if the speaker thinks there has been far too much talk.
fact: Palaver is an informal word, mostly used in everyday British talk.
fact: «Palaver» means a lot of fuss and bother about something that does not matter much.
fact: In old books, «palaver» means a long talk or a meeting between groups of people.
fact: Long ago, traders and local people held palavers to talk things over.
fact: «a palaver, and an indaba, and a punchayet, and a pow-wow» are four words from different lands that all mean a meeting to talk things over.
exercise: «What a palaver just to get a passport!» What does palaver mean here? | *A lot of fuss and bother | A quiet walk in the park | A picture of a passport | A happy holiday
exercise: In old adventure stories, a palaver is a meeting. What is it for? | To sleep under the stars | To race horses across the plains | To cook a big meal for the whole village | *To talk things over between groups of people
exercise: «They held a palaver, and an indaba, and a punchayet, and a pow-wow.» What do these four words have in common? | They all mean a long sleep | They all mean a kind of meal | *They all mean a meeting to talk things over | They all mean a loud race
test: Which sentence uses palaver to mean a fuss? | *Mum made a palaver of finding her keys, with everyone searching and shouting | The class sat in a circle for a palaver about the trip | The palaver was held by the river a long time ago | Palaver is a sweet you eat after dinner
test: Why might a writer call a long meeting a palaver? | To show that everyone was asleep | To show the meeting was short and quiet | To show the room was very cold | *To show there is too much talk and fuss
exam: After a long wait for the bus, a grown-up sighs, «What a palaver!» What does palaver mean? | A sunny day | *A fuss and bother about something | A happy song | A dog barking
